{"about":"Computed by tools/compute_limits.js from data/settings.json (limits) and the templates drawn by src/figure.js. Do not edit by hand.","status":"estimate","verdict":"Three limits come from the drawn body itself. Widen the average soldier at fixed bones and the arms are forced out first, the thighs close next, and the hands lose the face last.","method":"Each template is widened at fixed bones (every width and depth times the same factor; shoulder joints, hip joints and elbows stay put) until the limit is reached, and that shape's score is read with the measuring tool at 1,060 kg/m³ and 7.5% fat. The headline is the median soldier's shape; the band runs across six templates, from the 5th-percentile soldier to Ronnie Coleman's shape.","notes":["No study gives these limits. Each threshold is an estimate built from sourced pieces: what stops a joint (soft tissue meeting soft tissue), how far ordinary men can move, and how far daily tasks need. The sources are listed under “Where the body stops working” below.","This is flat, front-view geometry on drawn templates. Real soft tissue squashes, arms can sit a little in front of the body, and shoulders shrug, so each limit is a band, not a line.","A softer line comes earlier: with the elbow held to 120°, people still manage eating, drinking and washing by moving the shoulder and wrist instead (Vasen 1995).","The bands are for human-shaped bodies. Rex and Ollie are not built like the template: their art shows their arms well out and their thighs touching, so they are past the first two limits whatever their score."],"elbow_crease_fraction_of_upper_arm":0.4312,"definitions":{"arms_angle_deg":30,"thighs_fraction":1,"normal_max_flexion_deg":145,"max_flexion_deg":100,"density_kg_m3":1060,"fat_pct":7.5,"height_m":1.8},"templates":[{"id":"ansur2_m_p50","name":"US Army men, 50th percentile (ANSUR II)","score_at_f1":26.98,"elbow_flexion_at_f1_deg":145,"arm_rest_angle_at_f1_deg":7.7,"arms":{"f":1.2539,"score":42.42},"thighs":{